1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,780
Here's a shopping cart written in Objective-C, and its new Swift port.

2
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:09,060
Before the port ships, you want to know it behaves exactly like the original.

3
00:00:09,810 --> 00:00:14,150
The usual way is to write down what the original does: expected totals,

4
00:00:14,370 --> 00:00:16,270
mocks, one assertion per path.

5
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:18,370
With Haruspec, you don't.

6
00:00:18,610 --> 00:00:20,730
The original is the expected value.

7
00:00:21,130 --> 00:00:24,390
Both carts get the same inputs, and their behavior is compared.

8
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:29,470
What you write instead is a spec: how to interrogate the cart.

9
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:32,430
For this cart, its heart is three lines.

10
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:35,470
This video is about where everything else comes from.

11
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,040
It starts in your test target.

12
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:44,860
Paste the cart's interface into a protocol, initializer included, and stamp it with the Haruspec attribute.

13
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:49,100
Then declare that the original and the port both conform: one line each.

14
00:00:49,420 --> 00:00:51,720
The product code never imports Haruspec.

15
00:00:52,466 --> 00:00:53,626
The stamp is a macro.

16
00:00:54,146 --> 00:00:56,986
From that one protocol, it generates three things.

17
00:00:57,186 --> 00:01:03,106
A forwarder, which wraps either cart and records every call, its arguments and its return value.

18
00:01:03,506 --> 00:01:05,706
A catalog of operations to drive.

19
00:01:05,886 --> 00:01:10,446
And a stand-in, for when some other class takes a cart as a collaborator.

20
00:01:10,826 --> 00:01:12,146
That's the seams video.

21
00:01:12,586 --> 00:01:14,506
Here, the cart is the subject.

22
00:01:16,533 --> 00:01:18,873
The catalog comes straight from the signatures.

23
00:01:19,273 --> 00:01:21,553
Every method becomes an operation.

24
00:01:21,933 --> 00:01:24,453
Every argument gets a domain from its type.

25
00:01:25,083 --> 00:01:29,363
A whole number gets a ladder of magnitudes, out to ten thousand both ways,

26
00:01:29,743 --> 00:01:33,943
stepping either side of each power of ten, where off-by-one bugs live.

27
00:01:34,363 --> 00:01:36,683
A Boolean gets both values.

28
00:01:36,983 --> 00:01:40,823
A model, like the product, is built by its own generator.

29
00:01:41,063 --> 00:01:46,543
And each operation repeats zero to three times, so even a checkout on an empty cart

30
00:01:46,543 --> 00:01:47,243
gets tried.

31
00:01:47,703 --> 00:01:51,743
Any domain the engine chose is marked derived default on the verdict,

32
00:01:51,963 --> 00:01:54,903
so a green over guessed inputs says so.

33
00:01:55,485 --> 00:01:58,585
The effects worth observing come from the same paste.

34
00:01:58,805 --> 00:02:00,925
Calls and returns, at the forwarder.

35
00:02:01,345 --> 00:02:04,305
Notifications, through the center the initializer takes.

36
00:02:04,565 --> 00:02:09,445
And each property, read after every operation, so the path is compared,

37
00:02:09,725 --> 00:02:11,145
not just where it ended.

38
00:02:13,167 --> 00:02:14,127
Strings get nothing.

39
00:02:14,407 --> 00:02:19,647
A string's interesting values are magic words, like a promo code, and random text only ever

40
00:02:19,647 --> 00:02:21,247
reaches the branch where nothing matches.

41
00:02:21,747 --> 00:02:23,587
A green from that would be hollow.

42
00:02:23,987 --> 00:02:28,987
So a string stays pending, and binding the spec traps, naming the line to write.

43
00:02:29,737 --> 00:02:31,597
That's what the three lines are.

44
00:02:32,237 --> 00:02:37,417
Amend apply promo, varying the code over save ten, the same in lowercase,

45
00:02:37,997 --> 00:02:41,897
and nope: a match, a case-insensitive match, and no match.

46
00:02:42,397 --> 00:02:46,337
Amend the remove operation, varying its product code over the catalogue.

47
00:02:46,837 --> 00:02:49,357
And amend checkout: it runs last.

48
00:02:50,035 --> 00:02:57,415
The rest shapes the orderings: run once, repeat, follow another operation, which exploration still breaks on

49
00:02:57,415 --> 00:03:00,155
purpose, or require one, which it never does.

50
00:03:00,435 --> 00:03:05,535
When the call itself must differ, a full operation in the block replaces its derived twin.

51
00:03:07,567 --> 00:03:09,547
The spec names no implementation.

52
00:03:10,007 --> 00:03:11,547
It describes the protocol.

53
00:03:11,887 --> 00:03:14,767
The test chooses what to trust, with baselined on.

54
00:03:15,517 --> 00:03:17,257
Hand it the original's type.

55
00:03:17,617 --> 00:03:24,097
It's built through the pasted initializer, each collaborator resolved for you, and wrapped in the forwarder.

56
00:03:24,417 --> 00:03:29,897
Then each port is one check, by its type, built exactly the same way.

57
00:03:30,337 --> 00:03:34,657
A port whose dependencies differ is checked through its own initializer instead.

58
00:03:35,407 --> 00:03:39,807
A check that should go red says so: expecting diverged.

59
00:03:40,027 --> 00:03:42,547
A kept bug proves the oracle still bites.

60
00:03:43,297 --> 00:03:46,737
And the spec's name comes from the stamp: Cart.

61
00:03:46,997 --> 00:03:48,957
It titles reports and plans.

62
00:03:49,377 --> 00:03:52,737
Only when two specs interrogate one protocol do you name each.

63
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:56,560
Here's what the engine does with it.

64
00:03:56,820 --> 00:04:01,160
First, the original runs once, to learn which values the cart reads.

65
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:06,820
Then it builds orderings, sequences of operations, and pairs each with seam variants,

66
00:04:07,060 --> 00:04:08,380
fresh sets of those values.

67
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:12,260
Every pair runs on both carts, with identical inputs.

68
00:04:13,002 --> 00:04:18,102
The space is enormous, so a derived spec spends a budget: about twelve hundred runs,

69
00:04:18,282 --> 00:04:20,182
over at most fifty orderings.

70
00:04:20,522 --> 00:04:25,802
Where it can't buy everything, both axes shrink together, because they find different bugs.

71
00:04:26,402 --> 00:04:30,762
Edge values go first, so a small budget drops random middles, not ends.

72
00:04:31,382 --> 00:04:32,522
Vary changes any of it.

73
00:04:33,156 --> 00:04:38,496
And when a run goes red, the engine shrinks it, dropping operations one at a time

74
00:04:38,496 --> 00:04:40,156
while it still diverges.

75
00:04:40,456 --> 00:04:42,676
Reading that verdict is its own video.

76
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:48,460
That's the campaign shape: two implementations, one build.

77
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:51,740
The same spec also drives the continuous shape.

78
00:04:51,920 --> 00:04:56,340
Put it in a type and mark it continuous, naming the implementation to watch.

79
00:04:57,020 --> 00:05:01,520
Its suite records that behavior on main as a plan, and replays it against every branch.

80
00:05:02,270 --> 00:05:04,310
So, the one thing you write.

81
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:06,310
Paste and stamp the protocol.

82
00:05:06,710 --> 00:05:08,570
Amend what a signature can't say.

83
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:11,950
Let the test pick the baseline, and check each port.

84
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:13,810
Three lines, for a cart.

85
00:05:14,290 --> 00:05:16,850
What the cart talks to is the seams video.

86
00:05:17,130 --> 00:05:19,390
What comes back is the verdicts video.
